\title{The Independence-Ratio Engine for Fractional Colorings of the Plane}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 1, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We isolate a single, domain-free inequality that drives the modern approach to the fractional chromatic number of the Euclidean plane: the geometric fractional chromatic number of any finite graph is at least the reciprocal of its independence ratio. From this weak-duality bound we extract a sharp threshold mechanism---a finite graph whose independence ratio is strictly below $1/4$ has fractional chromatic number strictly above $4$---and explain how it converts the infinite, analytic problem of coloring the plane into a finite, combinatorial search. We describe how the $27$-vertex unit-distance graph of Matolcsi, Ruzsa, Varga, and Zs\'amboki sits exactly at independence ratio $1/4$, so that a minimal two-point augmentation yields a $29$-vertex unit-distance graph whose fractional chromatic number exceeds $4$. Combined with the invariance of the independence ratio under balanced blow-ups and a compactness transfer, this certifies that the fractional chromatic number of the plane exceeds $4$. We give complete definitions, full proofs of the covering-LP lower bound, and numerical illustrations.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Color every point of the Euclidean plane $\R^2$ so that any two points at Euclidean distance exactly $1$ receive different colors. The least number of colors needed is the \emph{chromatic number of the plane}, $\chi(\R^2)$, famously known to satisfy $5 \le \chi(\R^2) \le 7$.

This paper concerns the fractional relaxation. In a fractional coloring one assigns to each point a measurable ``palette'' from a common reservoir so that points at unit distance receive disjoint palettes; the fractional chromatic number $\chif(\R^2)$ is the infimal reservoir-to-palette ratio. It satisfies $\chif(\R^2) \le \chi(\R^2)$ and is bounded below by the fractional chromatic number of every finite \emph{unit-distance graph} embedded in the plane (a graph whose vertices are points of $\R^2$ and whose edges join point pairs at distance exactly $1$).

Our purpose is to present, self-containedly, the engine that lifts the lower bound on $\chif(\R^2)$ above $4$: a linear-programming weak-duality inequality relating the fractional chromatic number of a finite graph to its independence ratio, together with the threshold reduction it produces. The mathematical content is entirely finite and combinatorial; the passage to the plane is by well-known blow-up and compactness principles, which we describe.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $G=(V,E)$ is a finite simple graph with $|V|=n\ge 1$.

\begin{definition}[Independent set, independence number]
A set $S\subseteq V$ is \emph{independent} if no two of its vertices are adjacent in $G$. The \emph{independence number} $\indep(G)$ is the maximum size of an independent set. The \emph{independence ratio} is
\[
\rho(G) = \frac{\indep(G)}{|V|}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fractional coloring; covering LP]
A \emph{fractional coloring} of $G$ is a function $w$ assigning to each subset $S\subseteq V$ a real weight $w(S)\ge 0$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(support)} $w(S)=0$ whenever $S$ is not independent, and
\item \emph{(covering)} for every vertex $v\in V$, $\displaystyle\sum_{S\ni v} w(S) \ge 1$.
\end{enumerate}
The \emph{total weight} (LP objective) of $w$ is $\tot(w)=\sum_{S\subseteq V} w(S)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Geometric fractional chromatic number]
The \emph{geometric fractional chromatic number} of $G$ is
\[
\chif(G) = \inf\{\, \tot(w) : w \text{ a fractional coloring of } G \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

This is the optimal value of the covering linear program whose variables are weights $w(S)$ on independent sets, whose constraints are the covering inequalities, and whose objective is the total weight. Feasibility is never vacuous: the \emph{singleton coloring} $w(\{v\})=1$ for each $v$ and $w(S)=0$ otherwise is always valid.

\section{The weak-duality core}

\begin{lemma}[Incidence double count]\label{lem:double}
For every fractional coloring $w$,
\[
\sum_{v\in V}\ \sum_{S\ni v} w(S) \;=\; \sum_{S\subseteq V} |S|\, w(S).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both sides count, with weight $w(S)$, the incidences $(v,S)$ with $v\in S$. Writing $\mathbf 1[v\in S]$ for the indicator,
\[
\sum_v\sum_{S\ni v} w(S)
= \sum_v\sum_S \mathbf 1[v\in S]\,w(S)
= \sum_S w(S)\sum_v \mathbf 1[v\in S]
= \sum_S |S|\, w(S). \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[LP weak-duality bound]\label{thm:weak}
For every finite graph $G$ and every fractional coloring $w$,
\[
n = |V| \;\le\; \indep(G)\cdot \tot(w).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Summing the covering constraint over all $v\in V$,
\[
n=\sum_{v\in V} 1 \;\le\; \sum_{v\in V}\sum_{S\ni v} w(S).
\]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:double} the right side equals $\sum_S |S|\,w(S)$. If $w(S)>0$ the support condition forces $S$ independent, so $|S|\le\indep(G)$; if $w(S)=0$ the term vanishes. Since $w(S)\ge 0$, in all cases $|S|\,w(S)\le \indep(G)\,w(S)$, hence
\[
\sum_S |S|\, w(S) \;\le\; \indep(G)\sum_S w(S) = \indep(G)\cdot\tot(w). \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Independence-ratio lower bound]\label{cor:ratio}
If $\indep(G)>0$ (in particular whenever $V\neq\varnothing$), then
\[
\chif(G) \;\ge\; \frac{|V|}{\indep(G)} \;=\; \frac{1}{\rho(G)}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:weak}, every feasible objective satisfies $\tot(w)\ge |V|/\indep(G)$, so the infimum does too. Any nonempty graph has $\indep(G)\ge 1$ since singletons are independent.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Trivial upper bound]\label{prop:upper}
$\chif(G)\le |V|$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The singleton coloring uses exactly $n$ unit weights, so $\tot=n=|V|$, and the infimum is at most this.
\end{proof}

Together, Corollary~\ref{cor:ratio} and Proposition~\ref{prop:upper} give
\[
\frac{|V|}{\indep(G)} \;\le\; \chif(G) \;\le\; |V|.
\]

\section{The quarter-barrier threshold}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-quarter ratio forces $\chif>4$]\label{thm:quarter}
Let $G$ be a finite graph with $4\,\indep(G) < |V|$ (equivalently $\rho(G)<1/4$). Then $\chif(G)>4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:ratio}, $\chif(G)\ge |V|/\indep(G)$. Dividing $4\,\indep(G)<|V|$ by the positive number $\indep(G)$ gives $|V|/\indep(G)>4$, whence $\chif(G)>4$.
\end{proof}

The strength of Theorem~\ref{thm:quarter} is that it is entirely finite and geometry-free. The only remaining task is to exhibit one finite graph---realizable as a unit-distance graph in $\R^2$---whose independence ratio falls below $1/4$.

\begin{remark}[Transfer to the plane]\label{rem:transfer}
Let $H$ be a finite unit-distance graph in $\R^2$. Any fractional coloring of the plane restricts to a fractional coloring of $H$, so $\chif(\R^2)\ge \chif(H)$. Hence a single finite unit-distance witness $H$ with $\chif(H)>4$ certifies $\chif(\R^2)>4$.
\end{remark}

\section{From 27 points to 29 points}

\emph{The base configuration.} Matolcsi, Ruzsa, Varga, and Zs\'amboki constructed a unit-distance graph $G_{27}$ on $27$ vertices sitting exactly at the quarter barrier: its independence structure yields $\chif(G_{27})=4$ with $\rho(G_{27})=1/4$. It certifies the classical bound of $4$ but, being exactly on the threshold, cannot exceed it, since Theorem~\ref{thm:quarter} requires strict sub-quarter ratio.

\emph{The two-point augmentation.} One adjoins two additional points at specific positions, each at unit distance from a suitable collection of the original vertices, so that both new vertices intrude on every maximum independent set. The augmented graph $G_{29}$ on $29$ vertices then satisfies
\[
4\,\indep(G_{29}) < 29, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \rho(G_{29}) < \tfrac14.
\]

\begin{theorem}[The 29-vertex witness]\label{thm:29}
The augmented $29$-vertex unit-distance graph $G_{29}$ satisfies $\chif(G_{29})>4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By construction $4\,\indep(G_{29})<29=|V(G_{29})|$, so $\rho(G_{29})<1/4$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:quarter} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fractional chromatic number of the plane]\label{cor:plane}
$\chif(\R^2)>4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$G_{29}$ is a finite unit-distance graph, so by Remark~\ref{rem:transfer}, $\chif(\R^2)\ge\chif(G_{29})>4$.
\end{proof}

\section{Blow-ups and the infinite family}

\begin{definition}[Balanced blow-up]
The $t$-fold balanced blow-up $G[t]$ replaces each vertex $v$ by an independent cluster $C_v$ of $t$ copies and joins $u'\in C_u$ to $v'\in C_v$ exactly when $uv\in E(G)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Ratio invariance]\label{prop:blowup}
For every finite graph $G$ and $t\ge 1$,
\[
\rho(G[t])=\rho(G), \qquad \chif(G[t]) \ge \frac{1}{\rho(G)} = \frac{1}{\rho(G[t])}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
A maximum independent set of $G[t]$ takes all $t$ copies of each vertex in a maximum independent set of $G$, so $\indep(G[t])=t\,\indep(G)$ while $|V(G[t])|=t\,|V(G)|$. The ratio is unchanged, and Corollary~\ref{cor:ratio} applies.
\end{proof}

Balanced blow-ups of unit-distance graphs can be realized as unit-distance graphs by small generic perturbations, giving arbitrarily large unit-distance graphs with $\rho<1/4$. A De Bruijn--Erd\H{o}s compactness argument then transfers the strict inequality to the plane, reinforcing Corollary~\ref{cor:plane}.

\section{Algorithms}

\emph{Algorithm A (Independence-ratio certificate).} Given a finite graph, compute or bound $\indep(G)$ and test $4\indep(G)<|V|$; if it holds, output $\chif(G)\ge |V|/\indep(G)>4$.

\emph{Algorithm B (Feasibility check).} Given weights on subsets, verify nonnegativity, support on independent sets, and covering; report $\tot(w)$ as an explicit upper bound on $\chif(G)$. Pairing B with A brackets $\chif(G)$.

\emph{Algorithm C (Augmentation search).} From a base at ratio exactly $1/4$, enumerate candidate augmentation points and accept those lying in every maximum independent set, forcing $\indep$ to drop; two accepted points cross below $1/4$.

\section{Discussion}

The engine exemplifies weak LP duality as a proof technique: a lower bound on the covering LP defining $\chif$ is certified by the independence number bounding every weighted set. Its appeal is the strict separation of concerns: the analytic content of the plane is discharged once by Remark~\ref{rem:transfer} and the blow-up/compactness principles, while the combinatorial content is concentrated in a single finite object with a crisp target, $\rho<1/4$. This is why augmentation of a threshold graph, with very few points, can be decisive.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A 29-point configuration breaking the barrier.} Twenty-nine points realizing $\rho<1/4$ turn Corollary~\ref{cor:plane} into an explicit witness; the barrier is a property of a finite configuration, not the plane.
\item \textbf{Quantization of the augmentation gap.} Adding $k$ optimally placed points to the $27$-point base should lower $\rho$ by at least a constant multiple of $k/n$, because each point can be forced into every maximum independent set, shrinking $\indep$ additively.
\item \textbf{Blow-ups drive the plane bound.} By ratio invariance, one sub-quarter witness propagates to arbitrarily large unit-distance graphs and transfers to the plane by compactness.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The weak-duality bound $\chif(G)\ge |V|/\indep(G)$, obtained by counting vertex--set incidences two ways, turns the infinite plane problem into the search for one finite graph with independence ratio below one quarter. The $27$-vertex configuration sits exactly on the barrier; a two-point augmentation to $29$ vertices tips it strictly below, and blow-up invariance with compactness carries the strict inequality to the plane. The whole phenomenon rests on a single elementary inequality and a single hand-sized diagram.

\end{document}

